            the rule's filters and mood are applied. The following
            <InlineCode code="Violation found" language="text" /> line is where this candidate is
            confirmed as forbidden; it keeps the source, target, accumulated raw evidence, and
            negated mood. The full raw run also contains the self-edges that preserve import-free
            files, although this excerpt focuses on the forbidden UI-to-database dependency.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>

      <div class="graph-contract">
        <p class="deep-kicker">Raw graph versus projected graph</p>
        <h3>
          Projection changes the viewing scale for one question. It does not discover new
          dependencies.
        </h3>
        <p>
          The raw graph is the adapter's file-level evidence. A rule may need to ask about those
          exact files, or it may need a coarser view such as folders, layers, or business slices. A
          projection is the small mapping step between those two levels. It can discard edges that
          are irrelevant to the question, assign labels to each endpoint, and group edges that now
          have the same label pair.
        </p>

        <div
          class="projection-example"
          aria-label="Two raw file dependencies projected into one slice dependency"
        >
          <div>
            <span>Raw file facts</span>
            <InlineCode code="orders/api.py → payments/client.py" language="text" />
            <InlineCode code="orders/jobs.py → payments/client.py" language="text" />
          </div>
          <b aria-hidden="true">become</b>
          <div>
            <span>One slice-level candidate</span>
            <InlineCode code="Orders → Payments" language="text" />
            <p>
              <InlineCode code="cumulated_edges" language="python" /> still contains both raw file
              edges.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <p>
          In this grouping example, neither contributing import is lost. The projected edge gives
          the rule one <InlineCode code="Orders → Payments" language="text" /> candidate, while its
          accumulated evidence still identifies the two exact imports to report when the rule fails.
          A different projection may deliberately skip edges outside its question. This is why the
          model needs both a raw graph and a projected graph.
        </p>

        <ol class="projection-steps">
          <li>
            <strong>Map or skip.</strong> A mapper returns labels for a raw edge or no value when
            that edge is irrelevant.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Group equal labels.</strong> Edges with the same projected source and target
            become one candidate.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Retain proof.</strong> Every contributing raw edge remains attached for
            diagnostics and reports.
          </li>
        </ol>

        <div class="projection-language-notes">
          <p>
            <strong>Python file dependency rules:</strong>
            <InlineCode code="per_internal_edge()" language="python" /> removes external and
            self-edges, keeps the full paths as labels, and groups equal pairs.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>TypeScript file dependency rules:</strong>
            <InlineCode code="perEdge()" language="typescript" /> keeps each raw edge's labels and
            groups duplicate pairs. It does not automatically remove external or self-edges. The
            real trace's 132 to 124 change is grouping, not a second scan.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="stage-output">
          <span>Output of this chapter</span>
          <p>
            A raw edge list containing resolved facts, plus a question-specific list of projected
            candidates whose accumulated edges preserve the underlying proof.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="deep-section deep-rule-section" id="fluent-rule">
    <div class="deep-shell">
      <div class="deep-section-heading deep-section-heading--wide">
        <p class="deep-kicker">Chapter 04 / Turn intent into a fluent rule</p>
        <h2>The fluent API is a sentence-shaped plan, not the graph itself.</h2>
        <p>
          Building the chain records a subject, a mood, a predicate, an object, and optionally a
          rationale. Expensive work begins only at the terminal
          <InlineCode code="check()" language="python" /> or the test-framework assertion.
        </p>
        <p>
          Chapters 02 and 03 use the language selector because discovery, parsing, and resolution
          are adapter-specific. From this chapter onward, the machine is shared. Python and
          TypeScript implementation differences are labeled explicitly; unavailable adapters are not
          inferred from another language.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div
        class="sentence-machine"
        aria-label="An architecture sentence broken into fluent API stages"
      >
        <div>
          <span>entry</span>
          <strong>project files</strong>
        </div>
        <b>→</b>
        <div>
          <span>subject</span>
          <strong>in UI</strong>
        </div>
        <b>→</b>
        <div>
          <span>mood</span>
          <strong>should not</strong>
        </div>
        <b>→</b>
        <div>
          <span>predicate</span>
          <strong>depend on files</strong>
        </div>
        <b>→</b>
        <div>
          <span>object</span>
          <strong>in DB</strong>
        </div>
        <b>→</b>
        <div>
          <span>terminal</span>
          <strong>check</strong>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="deep-code-pair">
        <div class="deep-code">
          <p>
            <span>Python</span> The sentence and its architectural reason
          </p>
          <CodeWindow code={pythonRule} filename="test_architecture.py" language="python" copy />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-code">
          <p>
            <span>TypeScript</span> The real fixture rule with debug logging
          </p>
          <CodeWindow
            code={typescriptRule}
            filename="architecture.spec.ts"
            language="typescript"
            copy
          />
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="rule-parts">
        <article>
          <span>Subject filters</span>
          <p>Select source labels. Multiple scope filters combine to narrow the population.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <span>Mood</span>
          <p>
            <InlineCode code="should_not()" language="python" /> sets negated semantics. It does not
            mean that violations pass.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <span>Object filters</span>
          <p>
            Select dependency targets. For this rule, a matching directed edge is forbidden
            evidence.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <span>Rationale</span>
          <p>
            <InlineCode code="because()" language="python" /> travels to the assertion message so
            policy and evidence stay together.
